==Icelandic==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|is|non|akr}}, from {{inh|is|gem-pro|*akraz}}, from {{inh|is|ine-pro|*h₂éǵros||field}} whence also the Danish {{m|da|ager}}, Faroese {{m|fo|akur}}, Swedish {{m|sv|åker}} and Norwegian {{m|no|åker}}.

Germanic cognates include {{cog|ang|æcer}} ({{cog|en|acre}}), {{cog|ofs|ekker}}, {{cog|osx|akkar}} ({{cog|nl|akker}}), {{cog|goh|ackar}} ({{cog|de|Acker}}) and {{cog|got|𐌰𐌺𐍂𐍃}}. Other cognates include {{cog|grc|ἀγρός||sc=polytonic}}, Mycenaean Greek {{m|gmy|𐀀𐀒𐀫}}, {{cog|la|ager}} (Spanish {{m|es|agreste}}), Umbrian {{m|xum|ager}}, Old Armenian {{m|xcl|արտ}} and Sanskrit {{m|sa|अज्र|tr=ájra}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈaːk(ʰ)ʏr/|lang=is}}
*: {{rhymes|lang=is|aːkʏr}}

===Noun===
{{is-noun|m|akurs|akrar}}

# {{lb|is|agriculture}} [[field]]

====Declension====
{{is-decl-noun-m-s1||a|k|u|r}}

====Derived terms====
* {{l|is|fara eins og logi yfir akur}}
* {{l|is|akuryrkja}}

